1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,589
You reboot a server to fix a problem.

2
00:00:02,609 --> 00:00:07,067
And with it go the very logs that would have told you what the problem was.

3
00:00:07,087 --> 00:00:10,471
By default, some systems keep the journal only in memory.

4
00:00:10,941 --> 00:00:13,225
In this video: preserve system journals.

5
00:00:17,745 --> 00:00:20,328
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:20,798 --> 00:00:22,292
Preserve system journals.

7
00:00:22,762 --> 00:00:24,902
We'll see where the journal is stored,

8
00:00:24,922 --> 00:00:27,800
make it persistent with a small configuration file,

9
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:29,846
and check that it survives a reboot.

10
00:00:39,366 --> 00:00:41,714
The journal can live in two places.

11
00:00:41,734 --> 00:00:46,856
In memory, in slash run slash log slash journal, it is lost at every reboot.

12
00:00:46,876 --> 00:00:50,781
On disk, in slash var slash log slash journal, it is kept.

13
00:00:59,251 --> 00:01:02,078
The setting is called Storage. Auto means:

14
00:01:02,098 --> 00:01:07,231
keep it on disk only if the folder slash var slash log slash journal exists.

15
00:01:07,251 --> 00:01:12,248
Persistent means: always keep it on disk, and create the folder if needed.

16
00:01:12,268 --> 00:01:14,079
Volatile means memory only.

17
00:01:28,549 --> 00:01:33,265
The best way to be sure is to say persistent explicitly. That is what we will do.

18
00:01:38,785 --> 00:01:43,716
First, where is our journal? ls dash d lists the two folders themselves.

19
00:01:43,736 --> 00:01:48,323
Both exist here, so this machine already keeps the journal on disk.

20
00:01:48,343 --> 00:01:52,930
journalctl dash dash disk dash usage shows how much space it takes:

21
00:01:52,950 --> 00:01:54,168
sixteen megabytes.

22
00:02:02,638 --> 00:02:04,185
Now the configuration.

23
00:02:04,205 --> 00:02:10,096
On older systems you edit the file slash etc slash systemd slash journald dot conf.

24
00:02:10,116 --> 00:02:14,512
On this system that file does not exist: ls says no such file.

25
00:02:14,532 --> 00:02:20,922
The defaults live in slash usr slash lib, and there Storage is commented out, set to auto.

26
00:02:34,442 --> 00:02:36,566
We do not edit the default file.

27
00:02:36,586 --> 00:02:41,190
We add a small file of our own in the folder journald dot conf dot d.

28
00:02:41,210 --> 00:02:45,814
mkdir dash p creates the folder, and it is fine if it already exists.

29
00:02:54,284 --> 00:02:56,092
Then printf writes two lines:

30
00:02:56,112 --> 00:03:00,818
the section name Journal in square brackets, and Storage equals persistent.

31
00:03:00,838 --> 00:03:02,898
The backslash n means a new line.

32
00:03:02,918 --> 00:03:06,806
The pipe sends the text to tee, which writes it into the file,

33
00:03:06,826 --> 00:03:10,776
and also shows it on the screen. Sudo is needed to write there.

34
00:03:26,296 --> 00:03:32,026
The service must read the new setting. systemctl restart systemd-journald does that.

35
00:03:32,046 --> 00:03:37,092
And journalctl dash dash flush moves anything still in memory to the disk.

36
00:03:45,562 --> 00:03:48,685
Now ls shows a folder named after the machine id:

37
00:03:48,705 --> 00:03:51,251
that is where the journal lives on disk.

38
00:03:51,271 --> 00:03:54,651
And dash dash list dash boots shows the current boot.

39
00:04:04,171 --> 00:04:07,607
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

40
00:04:09,127 --> 00:04:13,640
We reboot. Now list dash boots shows two boots: the old one and the new one.

41
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:17,278
The old logs are still there. And our file is still in place.

42
00:04:28,798 --> 00:04:32,156
Your turn. Create the folder journald dot conf dot d.

43
00:04:32,176 --> 00:04:35,279
Add a file there that sets Storage to persistent.

44
00:04:35,299 --> 00:04:38,911
Restart the journal service, reboot, and run the checker.

45
00:04:45,381 --> 00:04:47,132
Try before you open the solution.

46
00:04:52,652 --> 00:04:56,664
Next up: start, stop, and check the status of network services.

47
00:04:57,134 --> 00:05:00,037
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
